    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Surrealism aimed, in Breton's words, to resolve the previously contradictory conditions of dream and reality into an absolute reality, a super-reality, and Breton held it to be above all a revolutionary movement.</li><li>Two rival groups published manifestos in October 1924, Yvan Goll's on 1 October and Breton's on 15 October, and Breton's group won the right to the term.</li><li>The movement was repeatedly divided over politics and membership, yet it spread to many countries and influenced Abstract <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/expressionism">Expressionism</a>, the Beat writers, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">Theatre</a> of the Absurd and the French revolt of May 1968.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In 1938 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> Exposition Internationale du Surréalisme was lit only by a single <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/incandescent-light-bulb">light bulb</a> over a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> brazier, with 1,200 coal bags hung from the ceiling, and visitors were given flashlights to view the art.</p></aside>
    </section>

