    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1917</span><span class="rf-text">Guillaume Apollinaire uses the word surrealism in his notes for the ballet Parade, which premiers on 18 May</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1920</span><span class="rf-text">Breton and Soupault publish The Magnetic Fields, written with automatic techniques</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1924</span><span class="rf-text">Yvan Goll publishes his Manifeste du surréalisme on 1 October and Breton publishes his on 15 October</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1925</span><span class="rf-text">La Peinture Surrealiste, the first Surrealist exhibition, is held at Galerie Pierre in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1929</span><span class="rf-text">The second Surrealist manifesto excludes members unwilling to commit to collective action, and the excluded respond with the pamphlet Un Cadavre</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1936</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> International Surrealist Exhibition is organised by Herbert Read, with an introduction by Breton</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1938</span><span class="rf-text">The Exposition Internationale du Surréalisme opens at the Beaux-arts Gallery in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, and the Chilean group La Mandrágora is founded</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1966</span><span class="rf-text">André Breton dies, and Surrealist groups continue without him</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sigmund-freud"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sigmund-freud.jpg" alt="Sigmund Freud" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sigmund-freud">Sigmund Freud</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sigmund_Freud,_by_Max_Halberstadt_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Max Halberstadt, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The word surrealism was first coined in March 1917 by Guillaume Apollinaire. In a letter to Paul Dermée he wrote that, all things considered, it was better to adopt surrealism than supernaturalism, which he had first used. He then described Parade, the Ballets Russes work that premiered on 18 May 1917, as a kind of surrealism. Jean Cocteau wrote its one-act scenario and Erik Satie its music. Apollinaire also took the term up again as the subtitle of his play Les Mamelles de Tirésias, which was written in 1903 and first performed in 1917.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> scattered the writers and artists who had been based in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, and many became involved with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dada">Dada</a>, believing that excessive rational thought and bourgeois values had brought the conflict upon the world. During the war André Breton, who had trained in medicine and psychiatry, served in a neurological hospital where he used <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigmund-freud">Sigmund Freud</a>'s psychoanalytic methods with soldiers suffering from shell-shock. There he met the young writer Jacques Vaché, whose anti-social attitude and disdain for established artistic tradition he admired.</p><p>Back in Paris, Breton joined in Dada activities and started the literary journal Littérature with Louis Aragon and Philippe Soupault. They experimented with automatic writing, writing spontaneously without censoring their thoughts, and published the results and accounts of dreams in the magazine. Breton and Soupault published The Magnetic Fields in 1920.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aims">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aims</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Breton defined Surrealism in his 1924 manifesto as pure psychic automatism, by which one proposes to express, verbally, in writing or in any other manner, the real functioning of thought, in the absence of all control exercised by reason and outside all aesthetic and moral preoccupation. Its encyclopedic definition rested on a belief in the superior reality of certain neglected forms of association, in the omnipotence of dream and in the disinterested play of thought. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigmund-freud">Freud</a>'s free association and dream analysis were of crucial importance to the Surrealists, who embraced idiosyncrasy while rejecting the idea of an underlying madness. Dalí later proclaimed, "There is only one difference between a madman and me. I am not mad."</p><p>The Surrealists also stressed that elements not normally found together could be combined in one frame to produce illogical and startling effects. Breton took this idea of startling juxtapositions from a 1918 essay by the poet Pierre Reverdy, who held that the more distant and true the relationship between two juxtaposed realities, the stronger the image.</p><p>The group aimed to revolutionise human experience in its personal, cultural, social and political aspects, and to free people from false rationality and restrictive customs and structures. Breton proclaimed that the true aim of Surrealism was "long live the social revolution, and it alone!" At various times Surrealists aligned with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anarchism">anarchism</a>, and they looked to the Hegelian and Marxist dialectic.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="events">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Course of Events</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>By October 1924 two rival groups had formed. One, led by Yvan Goll, included Tristan Tzara, Francis Picabia and Pierre Reverdy; the other was led by Breton. Goll published his Manifeste du surréalisme on 1 October 1924 and Breton published his, through Éditions du Sagittaire, on 15 October. The two men clashed openly at the Comédie des Champs-Élysées, at one point literally fighting, and Breton won through tactical and numerical superiority. That year the Bureau of Surrealist Research was established and began publishing La Révolution surréaliste. In 1925 an autonomous group formed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a>, and the first Surrealist exhibition, La Peinture Surrealiste, was held at Galerie Pierre in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>.</p><p>In 1929 Breton asked Surrealists to assess their degree of moral competence, and the second manifesto excluded anyone reluctant to commit to collective action. The excluded members attacked Breton in the pamphlet Un Cadavre. In 1933 the Surrealists' assertion that a proletarian literature within a capitalist society was impossible led to their break with the Association des Ecrivains et Artistes Révolutionnaires and the expulsion of Breton, Éluard and Crevel from the Communist Party.</p><p>The 1936 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> International Surrealist Exhibition and the 1938 Exposition Internationale du Surréalisme in Paris followed. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> many artists fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>, and Breton went to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in 1941. After the war the Paris group, led by Breton, decided to embrace <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anarchism">anarchism</a> explicitly.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="people">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">People</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/pablo-picasso"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pablo-picasso.jpg" alt="Pablo Picasso" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/pablo-picasso">Pablo Picasso</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pablo_picasso_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Argentina. Revista Vea y Lea, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>André Breton led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> group and wrote its manifestos. His group came to include Paul Éluard, Benjamin Péret, René Crevel, Robert Desnos, Max Ernst, Salvador Dalí, Luis Buñuel, Man Ray, Hans Arp, Joan Miró, Yves Tanguy, Dora Maar and others. Breton admired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pablo-picasso">Pablo Picasso</a> and Marcel Duchamp and courted them to join, but they remained peripheral. Aragon and Philippe Soupault were with him from the days of Littérature, though Aragon left the group after committing himself to the French Communist Party in 1932.</p><p>Painters and sculptors joined as the mid-1920s went on, among them Giorgio de Chirico, Alberto Giacometti, Valentine Hugo, Méret Oppenheim, Toyen and Kansuke Yamamoto. André Masson's automatic drawings of 1923 are often used as the point at which the visual arts were accepted and the break from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dada">Dada</a> made. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a> group included E. L. T. Mesens, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rene-magritte">René Magritte</a>, Paul Nougé, Marcel Lecomte and André Souris. Dalí joined the group in 1929, and he and Magritte created the most widely recognised images of the movement.</p><p>Others were expelled or left. Antonin Artaud and Roger Vitrac, who co-founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">Theatre</a> Alfred Jarry, were expelled, and Dalí was considered by Breton and his associates to have betrayed and left Surrealism. Peggy Guggenheim married Max Ernst and promoted the work of other Surrealists. Breton declared Frida Kahlo an innate Surrealist painter after meeting her in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> in 1938.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="outcomes">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Outcomes</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Breton's victory over Goll in 1924 did not end the quarrels. From that moment the history of Surrealism remained marked by fractures, resignations and resounding excommunications, each Surrealist having a personal view of the issue and goals and accepting more or less the definitions laid out by Breton. Breton's group refused to put the proletarian struggle before radical creation, which made the late 1920s a turbulent time for the group and the Communist Party alike, and many people close to Breton, notably Aragon, left to work with the Communists.</p><p>The disunion of 1929 and 1930 had very little negative effect on Surrealism as Breton saw it, since Aragon, Crevel, Dalí and Buñuel remained true to group action for the time being. The success, or controversy, of Dalí and Buñuel's film L'Age d'Or in December 1930 drew new recruits. Breton himself later admitted that the centrality of automatic writing had been overstated, and collage and other methods were introduced.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, artists such as Arshile <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizhny-novgorod">Gorky</a>, Jackson Pollock and Robert Motherwell converged closely with the exiled Surrealists, albeit with some suspicion and reservations. Abstract <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/expressionism">Expressionism</a> grew directly out of the meeting of New York artists with European Surrealists, and until the emergence of Pop art Surrealism can be seen as the single most important influence on the sudden growth of American arts.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Breton died in 1966. Jean Schuster disbanded the original <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> Surrealist Group in 1969, but another Parisian group was formed later. The Group of Czech-Slovak Surrealists never disbanded and continues to publish its journal Analogon, and groups in Chicago, Leeds and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stockholm">Stockholm</a> have also continued. Jan Švankmajer of the Czech-Slovak group continues to make films and experiment with objects.</p><p>The Surrealist idea reached well beyond the arts. Anticolonial writers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/negritude">Négritude</a> movement in Martinique took up Surrealism as a revolutionary method, and their journal Tropiques was first published in 1941. In the 1960s the Situationist International was closely associated with Surrealism, and among the slogans painted on the walls of the Sorbonne in May 1968 were familiar Surrealist ones. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> the Orange Alternative, created in 1981 by Waldemar Fydrych, used Surrealist symbolism in its happenings during the Jaruzelski regime.</p><p>Writers of the Beat Generation, among them Philip Lamantia and Ted Joans, were influenced by Surrealism, and Allen Ginsberg cited Artaud as a direct influence on Howl. The critic Martin Esslin argued that the playwrights he grouped as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">Theatre</a> of the Absurd could trace similarities to the Surrealists. Surrealist art remains popular with museum visitors: in 2001 an exhibition at Tate Modern attracted over 170,000 visitors, and Surrealism Beyond Borders in 2021 and 2022 brought together work from 45 or more countries.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Surrealism was an art and cultural movement that developed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> as its most important centre. Its members sought to let the unconscious express itself, and Breton held it to be above all a revolutionary movement. Its groups quarrelled and divided, but it spread across the world and its groups still exist.</em></p>

    <section class="ty-sec" id="test-yourself" aria-labelledby="ty-title">
